description:
calendar.rs:234-242, event.rs:432-440, event_notification.rs:112-120 — each /set destroy path validates that every element of the destroy array is a JSON String before processing:

  if let Some(bad) = destroy_arr.iter().find(|v| !v.is_string()) {
      return Err(JmapError::invalid_arguments(format!(
          'destroy: every element must be a string Id; got {bad}'
      )));
  }

RFC 8620 §5.3 says destroy is an Id[]; non-string entries are out of contract. A naive implementation would either skip non-strings silently (misleading 'no errors' response) or process them as 'unknown id' (misleading notDestroyed entry with the JSON-formatted value as a fake id).

The choice here:
- Reject the whole call with method-level invalidArguments.
- Include the offending value in the error string so the client can debug.

This matches RFC 8620 §3.6.1 invalidArguments semantics and is the right call. A future contributor reading 'why don't we just skip non-strings' should see this preserved.

Cross-reference bd:JMAP-r3pg.18 — this was specifically reviewed and tested (calendar.rs:700-715).

DEFEND when reviewers suggest 'silently filter to strings'.
